    84	
    85	## Detailed Evaluation Against Book Rules (Questions 1–10)
    86	
    87	### 1. Never False
    88	* **Enacted Law vs Proposal**: Wkb status is stated precisely (in force since 1 January 2024 for new consequence class 1; renovations postponed without definitive date) ([`03-wkb-scope.txt`](file:///home/diablo/book19/resources/sources/03/excerpts/03-wkb-scope.txt)). CPR DoP obligations are correctly referenced under Regulation (EU) 305/2011 and harmonised standards EN 14351-1 and EN 1090-1 ([`03-cpr-duty.txt`](file:///home/diablo/book19/resources/sources/03/excerpts/03-cpr-duty.txt), [`03-harmonised-standards.txt`](file:///home/diablo/book19/resources/sources/03/excerpts/03-harmonised-standards.txt)).
    89	* **CBAM Distinctions**: The definitive regime date (1 January 2026) and the >50-tonne mass threshold are cited accurately ([`03-cbam.txt`](file:///home/diablo/book19/resources/sources/03/excerpts/03-cbam.txt)). The text explicitly clarifies that CBAM attaches at the external EU customs border, correctly warning that intra-EU Poland-to-NL transactions do not generate external CBAM import filings (`03-cbam-boundary`).
    90	* **Hurdles vs Market Prices**: Sourced facts are separated from required-price arithmetic. The €2,000 pre-order gate and €3,000 completion pack are labeled as *"test design, not observed market rates"* and *"required-price hurdles"*.
    91	* **Dates**: All dates match primary sources.
    92	
    93	### 2. Buyer and Purchase Event
    94	* **Payer**: Project manager or operations director at a Dutch general contractor building consequence-class-1 homes or small commercial units who directly imports Polish joinery.
    95	* **Purchase Event**: The pre-order quotation reconciliation stage, before purchase order release, when product references, declared performance (DoP), and installation manuals must align with the building quality plan.
    96	* **Regulation vs Demand**: The essay explicitly distinguishes between regulatory burden and buyer willingness to pay:
    97	  > *"They prove goods move, not that Dutch builders bought them directly, that files were deficient or that a separate budget exists."* ([`L43`](file:///home/diablo/book19/chapters/03-joinery-steel-dossier.html#L43))
    98	
    99	### 3. Economics & Arithmetic
   100	* **Founder Replacement Cost**: Sourced separately as €100/hr (300 hours = €30,000 in Year 1; €100,000 in Mature).
   101	* **Owner Operating Surplus**: Separated from founder compensation (-€23.6k in Year 1; +€120k in Mature).
   102	* **Working Capital & Risk**: Working capital is controlled by refusing title to goods, billing 50% in advance, maintaining a €25,000 reserve hurdle, and capping pre-evidence spend at €3,500, honoring the ≤€50k cash-at-risk constraint.
   103	* **Internal Consistency**: Arithmetic in the table reconciles across all lines (`Revenue - Delivery = Gross Margin; Gross Margin - Overheads - Founder = Operating Surplus`). (See Finding 1 for capacity overlap).
   104	
   105	### 4. Scalability
   106	* **Evolution of Delivery**:
   107	  * *Customer 1*: Founder-led field mapping across drawings, codes, and declarations.
   108	  * *Customer 5*: Standardized intake sheets, exception taxonomy, bilingual coordinator handling routine document chasing (>50% non-founder hours).
   109	  * *Customer 20*: Multi-factory supplier response mapping, recurring error patterns, coordinator handling 4 live orders concurrently.
   110	* **No Platform Hand-Waving**: Explicitly rejects software illusions:
   111	  > *"The accumulating asset is not an app... customers never need another login."* ([`L94`](file:///home/diablo/book19/chapters/03-joinery-steel-dossier.html#L94))
   112	
   113	### 5. Incumbents and Capture
   114	* **Incumbent Treatment**: 
   115	  * Eko-Okna's Dutch distributor portal and parameter certificate download library are cited directly from their website ([`03-eko-incumbent.txt`](file:///home/diablo/book19/resources/sources/03/excerpts/03-eko-incumbent.txt)).
   116	  * FENBRO's turnkey supply/install model and Kozijnen Unie's Dutch-speaking builder supply model are analyzed from primary sources ([`03-fenbro-model.txt`](file:///home/diablo/book19/resources/sources/03/excerpts/03-fenbro-model.txt), [`03-dutch-dealer.txt`](file:///home/diablo/book19/resources/sources/03/excerpts/03-dutch-dealer.txt)).
   117	* **Partner Margin Capture**: Directly addresses the risk that a Dutch quality consultant could bypass the founder after learning Polish supplier channels, providing realistic operational defenses.
   118	
   119	### 6. Capable Failure
   120	* **Structural Failure Mode**: Presents a non-straw-man failure scenario: The founder executes cleanly, but the supply chain standardizes around the new rules, factories enhance their Dutch document portals, quality assurers clarify checklists, and the startup is left holding only low-volume, high-liability edge cases where fees collapse.
   121	
   122	### 7. Dutch-Language Gate, Reader Constraints & Precedent Firm
   123	* **Language Gate**: Channel-specific Dutch dependence (site meetings, buyer liability discussions, quality assurer negotiations) is stated and answered with a fixed-fee Dutch quality consultant (*"paper, partner or fail"*).
   124	* **Reader Constraints**: Respected in full:
   125	  * A2 Dutch: Founder handles Polish factories and document engineering; Dutch consultant handles buyer/site meetings.
   126	  * Hands-on & alongside job: 300 founder hours in Year 1 (~6h/week).
   127	  * Capital: €25k reserve, pre-evidence test capped at €3,500, no goods/freight liability.
   128	  * No trade exam: No statutory assurer role claimed.
   129	  * Outside software: Physical iron evidence pack.
   130	* **Precedent Firm**: **FENBRO Sp. z o.o.** verified via UK Companies House (FENBRO LTD, company #16295546, directors Agnieszka Justyna Brodzik and Pawel Brodzik at Ludna 2/320, Warsaw). Transparently notes the UK entity filed dormant accounts in April 2026 and sells bundled goods rather than standalone dossiers (`03-fenbro-trace`, `03-fenbro-dormant`, `03-precedent-gap`).
   131	
   132	### 8. Receipts & Claim Ledger
   133	* All 20 `<!-- CHECK: ... -->` markers in the HTML match rows in [`checks/claims/03.tsv`](file:///home/diablo/book19/checks/claims/03.tsv).
   134	* All 13 verified rows have verbatim text excerpts in [`resources/sources/03/excerpts/`](file:///home/diablo/book19/resources/sources/03/excerpts/).
   135	* All 4 inference rows (`03-cbam-boundary`, `03-gap-rate`, `03-language-gate`, `03-precedent-gap`) are properly reasoned.
   136	
   137	### 9. Verdict and Kill Assumption
   138	* **Verdict**: **WATCH** — accurately reflects that while Wkb creates documentation requirements, official guidance (TloKB) and incumbent portals (Eko-Okna) absorb standard compliance, making standalone willingness to pay uncertain.
   139	* **Kill Assumption**: Accurately targets the unproven willingness of a direct-import builder to pay a separate €5,000 evidence fee without bundling it into goods margins.
   140	
   141	### 10. Readability & Mechanics
   142	* **Word Count**: 2,884 words (within target range of 2,400–3,600).
   143	* **Sentences > 40 words**: 0.
   144	* **Paragraphs > 120 words**: 0.
   145	* **Jargon**: All acronyms (Wkb, DoP, CPR, EN 1090, CBAM, Bbl) are tied directly to operational mechanisms.
   146	
   147	---
   148	
   149	## The Three Fixes That Matter Most
   150	
   151	1. **Clarify Mature Technical Review Cost Allocation**: Reconcile the €60k external technical review line with the €90k in-house Dutch quality lead in the mature ledger table and narrative (Finding 1).
   152	2. **Clarify Year 1 Gate vs Pack Technical Review**: Specify that the €500 technical review applies to the pre-order intake gate rather than the completion pack (Finding 2).
   153	3. **Explicitly Bound Warehouse/Agricultural Prospect Scoping**: Add a sentence clarifying that agricultural/workshop builder outreach targets only their exterior window/door packages, keeping structural steel out of initial scope (Finding 3).
